Once go through it, this is the explanation why the old crs logic is not the best way to do this 

Your logic relies on PBKDF2 deriving a key from a "shared password". We have to ask: What exactly is this shared password, and where does it live?

Flaw 1: The "Shared Password" Trap (Critical Security Risk)

There are only two ways the frontend can have a "shared password" to mix with the salt:

• Scenario A: It is the user's actual login password. If you use the user's login password, the React frontend must keep the plaintext password in JavaScript memory for their entire 8-hour session so it can derive the key for every API call. ISD will instantly reject this. Storing plaintext passwords in browser memory makes them highly vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. Passwords must be hashed and forgotten the millisecond the login API completes.

• Scenario B: It is a hardcoded secret in the code.
If the frontend and backend share a hardcoded secret string (e.g., SBI_SECRET_2026), any hacker can open the browser's Developer Tools, look at your Vite JavaScript bundle, and extract the secret string. Because the salt and IV are sent in plaintext over the network, the hacker now has everything they need to derive the key and decrypt the payload.


Flaw 2: The Performance Penalty (PBKDF2 is intentionally slow)

PBKDF2 (Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2) is specifically designed to be slow and CPU-intensive. It uses "key stretching" (typically 100,000+ iterations) to prevent hackers from brute-forcing passwords.

• If your backend has to run 100,000 PBKDF2 iterations for every single incoming API request just to figure out the decryption key, your Spring Boot servers' CPU usage will spike to 100% almost immediately under banking loads.
• Contrast this with my suggested approach: Fetching a pre-made AES key from Redis takes < 1 millisecond, and AES-GCM decryption is hardware-accelerated.

 
Flaw 3: The "Transmission" Fallacy
Your teammate is worried about transmitting the AES key over the network during login. But remember, your application runs on HTTPS (TLS 1.2 or 1.3).
 

• Sending a 256-bit AES Session Key inside an HTTPS response payload is 100% mathematically secure.
• In fact, this is exactly how the internet works! When you visit a website, TLS uses asymmetric math (RSA/ECC) to securely transmit a symmetric session key, which is then used for the rest of the session. We are simply replicating this proven architecture at the application layer.

 
The Verdict: Why the Original Plan Wins
The Session Key approach (what we planned earlier) is the undisputed industry standard for End-to-End Payload Encryption in banking (often called E2EE).



